(defmodule languages
  "The non-Lisp language modes. `deflanguage` declares a tree-sitter language from ONE spec
(faces, indent, heredocs, formatter) and generates its whole service set — Ruby, Elixir,
Python, Rust, the brace languages, C#, CSS, HTML, go.mod, HCL / Terraform, shell, JSON, TOML,
YAML and Dockerfile are its clients, each a face table and an indent spec; the last five
keep their line lexer as the stand-in for a missing grammar (`:fallback`). Beside them, the
fontify-only layers over the `lex-*` modules: env, JSON5, Makefile, INI, git commit
messages and git's ignore and attributes files. Mail (mbox, .eml) is `mail`'s."
  (:use editor/layers) ; register-interpreter-type — shell-mode's shebang names
  (:use modes :only [register-language-mode register-prog-mode layer-commands])
  (:use interactive :only [defcommand])) ; org-mode's commands
;; editor/treesit, editor/highlight and the std lexers are referenced qualified below —
;; each `mod/name` auto-loads its module.

(defmacro deflanguage (name spec)
  "Declare a tree-sitter language mode from ONE spec, and register it.

    (deflanguage elixir
      {:lang :elixir
       :pattern \"[.](ex|exs)$\"
       :faces elixir-face-table
       :indent elixir-indent-spec
       :heredocs *elixir-heredocs*
       :comment \"# \"})

Every language used to spell out the same five definitions — a `-fontify`, a
`-fontify-restart`, an `-indent`, a layer naming all three, and the registration — of
which only the tables differed. Three copies is where a language quietly gets less than
the others: the mode was the place a service could be forgotten, and a forgotten service
is invisible until someone presses the key. Here a language supplies DATA and gets the
whole service set, or fails at load saying which part is missing.

The services are generated as named globals rather than closures because
`model/ed-mode-service` resolves a service SYMBOL late (`reflect/eval`) — which is what
lets a redefined fontify take effect on the next frame without reloading the mode. A
closure in the layer would be resolved once and frozen.

`:format` is the language's formatter as a command vector — `[\"mix\" \"format\"]`, to which
the file path is appended — or a vector of them, `[[\"terraform\" \"fmt\"] [\"tofu\" \"fmt\"]]`,
when several programs format the language: the first installed one runs
(`formatter/format-command-choice`). It becomes the `:format-command` service that `M-x
format-buffer` and format-on-save consult, so neither names a language.

`:definitions` says which nodes C-M-a / C-M-e / C-M-h treat as a definition —
`{:kinds #{:function_item …}}`, or `:calls` for a language that defines with a call
(`editor/treesit/register-definitions`). Ruby and Elixir are declared by std, whose
grammars they are; a language that declares nothing moves by top-level form.

`:grammar` is where the language's grammar lives — a git URL, or `{:url … :subdir …}` for
a repository holding several — so `M-x grammar-install` can fetch and build it, and
opening a file of the language without one says so (`grammars/declare-grammar`).

`:type` is the buffer type the mode registers as, when it is not the grammar's name: one
grammar may serve two modes that differ in something else — HCL's parses Terraform and
plain HCL, and only Terraform's files format with `terraform fmt`. Without it the type is
`:lang`, and a second mode over the same grammar would take the first one's files.

`:indent-tabs` true writes indentation as tabs (Go's gofmt'd sources), at the indenter's
column.

`:fallback` names a line lexer by its `deflexer` stem (`'lex-shell/shell`) for a language
that had one before it had a grammar. The grammar is a separate download, and a parsed mode
without it colours nothing; with a fallback, a buffer opened while the grammar is missing is
typed as the lexer's mode instead (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`), and becomes the parsed mode
when `M-x grammar-install` or `grammar-reload` brings the grammar (`model/ed-grammars-arrived`).
`:fallback-above` is a size in characters past which the lexer serves even with the grammar —
JSON's, where a held tree of a large machine-written file costs more than it buys.

`:injections` is the code EMBEDDED in the language (brood ADR-489): a vector of injection
rules, `[{:content {:kind :template_literal :parent :heredoc_template} :lang-from
:heredoc_identifier :aliases {\"BASH\" :bash}}]` — which nodes hold another language's text
and how its language is named. The embedded language's faces are its own `deflanguage`'s,
found when a frame asks (`spec-injections`); one with no spec or no grammar keeps this
language's colours. Checked at registration like the face table.

`:interpreters` lists the `#!` program names that select the mode for a file with no telling
name, as `deflexer`'s does.

`:fontify-restart` says where a windowed fontify may cut its lead-in, and comes from one of
three declarations. `:heredocs` (paired markers, Elixir's `\"\"\"`) restarts at a column-0 line
outside them (`editor/treesit/column0-restart`). `:restart {:item-start :opaque}` is the
brace languages' rule (`column0-item-restart`): the column-0 line that LOOKS like an item
start, unless something whose contents can be anything (a block comment, a raw string)
opens above it. `:restart 'sym` names a hand-written one when the rule is neither — Ruby
must also notice `=begin`. A language with none declines to restart, which is a fact about
the language — `editor/treesit/language-contract` says which services are required and
which are optional.

The generated restart is `<name>-restart`, not `<name>-fontify-restart`: the obvious name is
the one a language with a CUSTOM restart has already used, and generating it once silently
replaced Ruby's — which then answered \"restart anywhere\" and coloured the prose inside its
`=begin` blocks as code. A generated definition must not be able to take a hand-written
name.

The spec is evaluated ONCE, into `<name>-spec`, so a spec built from a shared base
(`(merge js-spec {…})`) is one value every generated service reads."
  (let (base (->string name)
        generated (fn (suffix) (symbol (str base suffix)))
        qualified (fn (suffix) (symbol (str "languages/" base suffix)))
        spec-name (generated "-spec")
        spec-ref (qualified "-spec"))
    `(do
       (def ~spec-name ~spec)
       (defn ~(generated "-fontify") (text)
         (editor/treesit/fontify text
           (get ~spec-ref :lang)
           (get ~spec-ref :faces)
           true
           (languages/spec-injections ~spec-ref)))
       ;; the embedded languages' injection spec, built per call: an injected language's
       ;; faces are ITS spec's, and it may be registered after this one
       (defn ~(generated "-ts-injections") () (languages/spec-injections ~spec-ref))
       ;; `tree`: the buffer's held tree for exactly `text`, when the caller has one
       ;; (`commands/ed-indent-target`) — the indenter walks it instead of parsing `text`, which
       ;; is then the buffer's rope: only the lines it inspects are read (brood ADR-483)
       (defn ~(generated "-indent") (text bol &optional (tree nil))
         (let (indent (get ~spec-ref :indent))
           (if (= indent :content)
             (languages/content-indent text bol)
             (editor/treesit/indent-column text (get ~spec-ref :lang) bol indent tree))))
       (defn ~(generated "-restart") (src pos)
         (languages/spec-restart ~spec-ref src pos))
       ;; show-paren over the parse: the view's window of text, or the held tree
       ;; (`:bracket-match-tree`, `tsbrackets/match-tree`) when the buffer keeps one
       (defn ~(generated "-bracket-match") (text pos)
         (tsbrackets/match-text text (get ~spec-ref :lang) pos))
       ;; the face table as a global the `:ts-faces` service names, so a held tree is
       ;; coloured with the table the fontify uses (`editor/treesit/fontify-tree`)
       (def ~(generated "-ts-faces") (get ~spec-ref :faces))
       (def ~(generated "-mode-layer")
         (languages/language-layer '~name
           ~spec-ref
           {:fontify '~(qualified "-fontify")
            :indent '~(qualified "-indent")
            :restart '~(qualified "-restart")
            :faces '~(qualified "-ts-faces")
            :injections '~(qualified "-ts-injections")
            :bracket-match '~(qualified "-bracket-match")}))
       ;; the line-lexer stand-in a buffer is typed as while the grammar is missing; nil
       ;; for a language with no `:fallback`
       (def ~(generated "-fallback-layer") (languages/fallback-layer '~name ~spec-ref))
       (languages/declare-definitions ~spec-ref)
       (languages/register-spec ~spec-ref '~spec-ref)
       (languages/declare-grammar ~spec-ref)
       (languages/register-fallback ~spec-ref '~(qualified "-fallback-layer"))
       (doseq (program (get ~spec-ref :interpreters []))
         (register-interpreter-type program (languages/spec-type ~spec-ref)))
       (register-prog-mode '~(qualified "-mode-layer") (languages/spec-type ~spec-ref)))))

(defn spec-type (spec)
  "The buffer type a `deflanguage` spec registers as: its `:type`, else its `:lang`."
  (or (get spec :type) (get spec :lang)))

(defn fallback-type (spec)
  "The buffer type of a `deflanguage`'s line-lexer stand-in — `:shell-lexer` for shell — or
nil for a language with no `:fallback`."
  (when (get spec :fallback) (keyword (str (->string (spec-type spec)) "-lexer"))))

(defn fallback-layer (name spec)
  "The layer a `deflanguage` with a `:fallback` lexer stem stands in with while its grammar
is missing (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`): today's line-lexer mode, under the same name, and
saying which grammar it wants (`:wants-grammar`) so `M-x grammar-install` offers it first.
It keeps the language's formatter: `shfmt` formats a script whether or not it was parsed.
Nil without a `:fallback`."
  (when (get spec :fallback)
    (assoc (lexer-layer name
             {:pattern (get spec :pattern) :lexer (get spec :fallback) :comment (get spec :comment)})
      :wants-grammar (get spec :lang)
      :format-command (get spec :format))))

(defn register-fallback (spec layer-ref)
  "Bind a `deflanguage`'s stand-in type to its fallback layer over text-mode. No file pattern
names the type: a buffer reaches it only as the stand-in for the parsed mode."
  (let (type (fallback-type spec))
    (when type (register-type-layers type (list layer-ref 'modes/text-mode-layer)))))

(defn content-indent (text bol)
  "The indentation a `deflanguage` with `:indent :content` gives the line starting at `bol`
of `text` (a string or a rope): its OWN, so TAB changes nothing — and, for a blank line
(the RET case), the previous non-blank line's, so the content's indentation continues.
YAML's: its columns are its data, and no tree can say where a line belongs.

    (languages/content-indent \"a:\\n    b: 1\\n\" 3)   ;=> 4
    (languages/content-indent \"a:\\n  b: 1\\n\\n\" 10)   ;=> 2"
  (let (rope (if (string? text) (text/from-string text) text)
        own (fn (line) (- (string/length line) (string/length (string/triml line))))
        blank? (fn (line) (= (string/trim line) ""))
        n (text/char->line rope bol)
        line (text/line rope n))
    (if (not (blank? line))
      (own line)
      (letrec (back (fn (i)
                      (cond
                        (< i 0) 0
                        (blank? (text/line rope i)) (back (dec i))
                        :else (own (text/line rope i)))))
        (back (dec n))))))

(defn spec-restart (spec src pos)
  "The `:fontify-restart` a `deflanguage` spec declares as data: a `:restart` map's item rule
(`column0-item-restart`), else `:heredocs`' paired-marker rule, else `pos` itself — which is
only reached for a language whose layer names no generated restart."
  (let (rule (get spec :restart)
        heredocs (get spec :heredocs))
    (cond
      (map? rule) (column0-item-restart src pos (:item-start rule) (:opaque rule))
      (not (nil? heredocs)) (editor/treesit/column0-restart src pos heredocs)
      else pos)))

(defn declare-definitions (spec)
  "Register a `deflanguage` spec's `:definitions` with `editor/treesit`. A spec without one
leaves std's declaration alone — Ruby's and Elixir's live there."
  (let (definitions (get spec :definitions))
    (when (map? definitions)
      (editor/treesit/register-definitions (get spec :lang) definitions))))

(defn declare-grammar (spec)
  "Record where a `deflanguage` spec's grammar lives (`:grammar` — a git URL, or `{:url
:subdir}` for a repository holding several), so `M-x grammar-install` can fetch it and a
file of the language opened without one can say how to get it."
  (let (grammar (get spec :grammar))
    (when (not (nil? grammar))
      (grammars/declare-grammar (get spec :lang) grammar))))

;; ---- injections: another language's code inside this one (brood ADR-489) ---------------
;; A spec's `:injections` is the RULES of an ADR-489 injection spec: which nodes hold which
;; language's text. The faces are not written there. Each embedded language colours with the
;; table its own `deflanguage` declares, looked up when a frame asks, so the shell inside a
;; Terraform heredoc follows shell-mode's table and a language registered later is found.

(defn register-spec (spec spec-ref)
  "Record that `spec-ref` (the `<name>-spec` global's symbol) is the spec of the tree-sitter
language `(:lang spec)`, where `language-faces` finds an embedded language's table. Stored
as the symbol and resolved per lookup, so a re-evaluated spec is the one read."
  (registry/put! :languages/specs (get spec :lang) spec-ref))

(defn language-faces (lang)
  "The face table of the `deflanguage` whose grammar is `lang`, or nil when none is
registered — an embedded language with no table keeps its host's colours."
  (let (spec-ref (registry/lookup :languages/specs lang))
    (when spec-ref (get (reflect/eval spec-ref) :faces))))

(defn- rule-languages (rule)
  ;; every language a rule can name: its fixed `:lang`, or its aliases' targets
  (if (get rule :lang) [(get rule :lang)] (vals (or (get rule :aliases) {}))))

(defn injection-spec (rules faces-of)
  "The injection spec `{:rules :faces}` for `rules`, each named language's table from
`(faces-of lang)`. A language with no table is left out: a rule fixed to it is dropped, an
alias to it removed. Nil when no rule is left, which a fontify reads as no injections."
  (let (faces (fold (mapcat rules rule-languages)
                {}
                (fn (acc lang)
                  (if (contains? acc lang)
                    acc
                    (let (table (faces-of lang)) (if table (assoc acc lang table) acc)))))
        kept (seq/keep rules
               (fn (rule)
                 (cond
                   (get rule :lang) (when (contains? faces (get rule :lang)) rule)
                   (get rule :aliases)
                     (assoc rule
                       :aliases
                         (into {}
                           (seq/filter (get rule :aliases)
                             (fn ([_ lang]) (contains? faces lang)))))
                   else rule))))
    (when (not (empty? kept)) {:rules (vec kept) :faces faces})))

(defn spec-injections (spec)
  "The injection spec a `deflanguage` spec's `:injections` rules make with the registered
languages' tables (`injection-spec`), or nil for a language that embeds none."
  (let (rules (get spec :injections))
    (when (not (empty? rules)) (injection-spec rules language-faces))))

(defn injection-rule-problems (rules)
  "What is malformed in a `deflanguage`'s `:injections` rules (`editor/treesit/injection-problems`),
each named language given an empty table: whether the language has a real one is a question
for the frame, when every language has registered."
  (let (placeholder (into {} (map (mapcat rules rule-languages) (fn (lang) [lang {}]))))
    (editor/treesit/injection-problems {:rules rules :faces placeholder})))

(defn language-layer (name spec refs)
  "The mode layer for a `deflanguage`, checked against `editor/treesit/language-contract`.
`refs` names the generated services: `{:fontify :indent :restart :faces :injections
:bracket-match}`.

Raises on an incomplete language rather than registering one. A mode missing `:indent`
would register happily and simply do nothing on RET — a failure that surfaces as a key
which feels broken, in one language, much later. A malformed face table is refused the
same way (`editor/treesit/face-table-problems`): the kernel would refuse it too, but at
the first frame drawn, and a misspelt selector key (`:feild`) would otherwise be found
by someone opening a file."
  (let (rule (get spec :restart)
        layer {:name name
               :ts-lang (get spec :lang)
               ;; the `{selector :syntax/face}` table, for colouring a HELD tree
               ;; (`syntaxtree`, brood ADR-443): `:fontify` takes text, and a view that
               ;; keeps the document's parse asks the tree with the same table instead
               :ts-faces (:faces refs)
               ;; a fn answering the injection spec the held tree is coloured with — the
               ;; same one `:fontify` passes — for a language whose spec embeds others
               :ts-injections (when (get spec :injections) (:injections refs))
               :file-pattern (get spec :pattern)
               :fontify (:fontify refs)
               :indent (:indent refs)
               ;; a hand-written one by name (Ruby's, which must also see `=begin`), the
               ;; generated one for a rule declared as data, or none — a language with no
               ;; safe cut declines, which is a fact about it rather than a hole in the mode
               :fontify-restart (cond
                                  (symbol? rule) rule
                                  (or (map? rule) (get spec :heredocs)) (:restart refs)
                                  else nil)
               :comment-syntax (get spec :comment)
               ;; `[open close]` for a language whose only comment is a block (CSS, HTML),
               ;; which a line prefix cannot write
               :comment-block (get spec :comment-block)
               ;; the bracket pair at point, from the parse: a `(` in a string matches nothing
               :bracket-match (:bracket-match refs)
               :bracket-match-tree 'tsbrackets/match-tree
               ;; The language's own formatter, as `[prog arg …]` with the file appended
               ;; — `mix format <file>`. A service like any other, so `M-x format-buffer`
               ;; and format-on-save ask the BUFFER what formats it and never name a
               ;; language. Absent for a language with no formatter, which is a fact
               ;; about the language.
               :format-command (get spec :format)
               ;; Go and C's tab-indented sources: the indenter answers a column, and a
               ;; mode that indents with tabs writes it as tabs (`commands/ed-indentation`)
               :indent-tabs (= true (get spec :indent-tabs))
               ;; the line-lexer type a buffer stands in with while the grammar is missing,
               ;; or past `:fallback-above` characters (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`)
               :grammar-fallback (when (fallback-type spec)
                                   {:type (fallback-type spec) :above (get spec :fallback-above)})}
        gaps (editor/treesit/missing-services layer)
        face-problems (editor/treesit/face-table-problems (get spec :faces))
        injection-problems (injection-rule-problems (get spec :injections [])))
    (when (not (empty? gaps))
      (error "deflanguage " name ": missing required service(s) " (->string gaps)))
    (when (not (empty? face-problems))
      (error "deflanguage " name ": malformed face table: " (string/join face-problems "; ")))
    (when (not (empty? injection-problems))
      (error "deflanguage "
        name
        ": malformed injections: "
        (string/join injection-problems "; ")))
    layer))

;; ---- the line-lexer modes: env, JSON5 and the configuration formats -------------------
;; A language with no grammar here is lexed a line at a time by a pure module (`lex-dotenv`,
;; `lex-configs`; shell's and Dockerfile's serve as `:fallback`s): its buffers keep every
;; text-mode binding and gain colouring. The `:file-pattern` is matched against the file's
;; basename, so a bare `.env` or `Makefile` triggers. Each restarts at the viewport's own line
;; (`editor/highlight/line-restart`: no cross-line state worth a backscan) and keeps each
;; line's spans as a memo (`:line-lexer`, brood ADR-471).

(defmacro deflexer (name spec)
  "Declare a line-lexer language mode from ONE spec, and register it — the sibling of
`deflanguage` for a language lexed line by line rather than parsed.

    (deflexer yaml
      {:pattern \"[.]ya?ml$\"
       :lexer 'lex-configs/yaml
       :comment \"# \"})

`:lexer` names the lexer by its stem: the module defines `<stem>-spans`, the whole-text
`:fontify`, and `<stem>-line-lexer`, the `:line-lexer` the pane's line cache threads.
`:comment` is the comment token `M-;` writes. `:interpreters` lists the `#!` program names
that select the mode for a file with no telling name (`register-interpreter-type`).

Nine layers spelled the same six keys by hand, and one of them wrote `:comment` where every
reader asks for `:comment-syntax` — so `M-;` in a shell script did nothing, silently. A
spec's keys are the language's facts; the layer's are the editor's, and only this maps one
to the other."
  (let (base (->string name)
        layer (symbol (str base "-mode-layer"))
        layer-ref (symbol (str "languages/" base "-mode-layer")))
    `(do
       (def ~layer (languages/lexer-layer '~name ~spec))
       (doseq (program (get ~spec :interpreters []))
         (register-interpreter-type program (keyword '~name)))
       (register-language-mode '~layer-ref (keyword '~name)))))

(defn lexer-layer (name spec)
  "The mode layer a `deflexer` spec declares: `:fontify` and `:line-lexer` from the `:lexer`
stem, the line restart, and `:comment-syntax`. Raises on a spec with no `:pattern` or
`:lexer`, which would register a mode no file reaches or one that colours nothing."
  (let ({:keys [pattern lexer comment]} spec)
    (when (not (and (string? pattern) (symbol? lexer)))
      (error "deflexer " name ": needs a :pattern string and a :lexer symbol: " (pr-str spec)))
    {:name name
     :file-pattern pattern
     :fontify (symbol (str lexer "-spans"))
     :line-lexer (symbol (str lexer "-line-lexer"))
     :fontify-restart 'editor/highlight/line-restart
     :comment-syntax comment}))

;; .env and its variants (.env.local, .env.production, …): the leading-dot name carries no
;; extension, so anchor on `.env` with an optional `.suffix`
(deflexer env
  {:pattern "(^|/)\\.env(\\.[^/]+)?$"
   :lexer 'lex-dotenv/env
   :comment "# "})

;; ---- ruby-mode / elixir-mode: tree-sitter-backed language modes -------------
;; The first modes whose parser is NOT Brood's reader: a `.rb`/`.ex` buffer gets
;; syntax colouring AND structural navigation from a tree-sitter grammar
;; (`tree-sitter-parse`), via `std/editor/treesit` — the foreign-language analogue
;; of brood-mode's `highlight`+`sexp`. The proof of ROADMAP §C: because the
;; builtin yields the SAME positioned-node shape Brood's reader does, the editor's
;; `:fontify` service and the `C-M-f/b/u/d/a` structural commands run over a Ruby
;; or Elixir tree unchanged — the modes are pure data (a face table + a `:ts-lang`
;; + a layer), and now an indent spec: `editor/treesit/indent-column` walks the tree and each
;; language names the node kinds that open a level (`languages/elixir-indent-spec`).
;;
;; `:fontify-restart` says where the windowed lead-in may be CUT. Without one the mode
;; parses the whole lead-in every keystroke — 245 lines for a 44-row viewport, five times
;; what is on screen, and 18 ms of a 25 ms keystroke on a 10k-line file. Elixir has one
;; (`editor/treesit/column0-restart`, guarded on heredoc parity — see there); Ruby does NOT,
;; deliberately: its heredoc terminator is arbitrary text (`<<~EOS`), so there is no paired
;; marker to count and no way to tell a column-0 line inside one from a real top-level form.
;; Guessing there would colour prose as code; Ruby keeps the lead-in until its own guard
;; exists.

;; (The `C-M-*` structural motions come from the shared `prog-mode-layer` above — ruby and
;; elixir stack it via `register-prog-mode`, and the commands dispatch on `:ts-lang`.)

;; Per-language face tables name only the *named* nodes to colour, by kind or by a kind in
;; its context (`{:parent … :field … :nth …}`, brood ADR-486); keywords (anonymous
;; alphabetic tokens — `def`/`end`/`do`/`fn`/…) come free, and the table is passed straight
;; to `editor/treesit/fontify`. Refine one by inspecting `(%tree-sitter-parse …)` — and a
;; field name by trying a `{:field …}` selector against `%tree-sitter-spans`.
;; ---- indentation for the tree-sitter modes ----------------------------------
;; `editor/treesit/indent-column` is the mechanism (walk the tree, count enclosing blocks);
;; these four entries are the whole per-language policy. The kinds were read off real parses
;; rather than guessed, and each spec is checked line-for-line against hand-written source —
;; every line of a sample module must re-indent to the column it already has.
;;
;; `:dedent-levels` differs because the grammars do: Elixir's `do_block` spans its own `end`,
;; so a closing line measured at the line start is still inside the block and backs out one;
;; Ruby's `body_statement` stops before `end`, so the closer is already outside and backs out
;; none.

(def elixir-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{:do_block :body} ; `do … end`, and a `->` clause's body
   ;; The bracketed openers — a multi-line list/map/tuple/binary/`fn` indents its
   ;; contents exactly as a block does, but must NOT indent its own opening line, which
   ;; is what `:brackets` means. `:arguments` is deliberately absent: Elixir does not add
   ;; a level for a call's argument list when the last argument is an `fn`, which is the
   ;; common shape (`Enum.reduce(xs, %{}, fn … end)`), and counting it put every such
   ;; clause one level too deep.
   :brackets #{:anonymous_function :list :map :tuple :bitstring}
   ;; keywords only; the closing delimiters decide for themselves
   :dedent #{"end" "else" "catch" "rescue" "after"}
   :dedent-levels 1})

(def ruby-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   ;; a method/class body, an if/else arm, and a BRACE block's body — `{ |x| … }` spanning
   ;; lines indents its contents like any block, and `:block_body` is the kind the grammar
   ;; gives it. Without it every multi-line `{ |t| … }` body sat a level too shallow, which
   ;; is 44 of the 46 lines the Ruby stdlib disagreed with.
   :blocks #{:body_statement :then :else :block_body}
   ;; the bracketed openers, which must not indent their own opening line
   :brackets #{:array :hash :argument_list}
   ;; Per token, because the grammar disagrees per token: a `body_statement` stops before
   ;; its `end`, so `end` is already outside it and backs out nothing — but it SPANS its
   ;; own `ensure`/`rescue`/`else`, so those must back out one. A single `:dedent-levels`
   ;; cannot say both, and with one number Ruby's `ensure` sat a level too deep in every
   ;; method that had one.
   ;; Keywords only — a closing delimiter decides for itself (see `ts-dedent-levels`),
   ;; because the same `}` closes a hash whose node encloses the closing line AND a
   ;; `{ |x| … }` block whose body ended before it. Read off the grammar, one token at a
   ;; time, against the Ruby stdlib:
   ;;   `end`                  a `body_statement` stops before it — already outside
   ;;   `else` `elsif` `when`  the arm ends before the keyword — already outside
   ;;   `rescue` `ensure`      children of the `body_statement`, which SPANS them
   :dedent {"end" 0 "else" 0 "elsif" 0 "when" 0 "rescue" 1 "ensure" 1}})

(def ruby-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :string :syntax/string
   :heredoc_body :syntax/string
   :regex :syntax/string
   :simple_symbol :syntax/special
   :hash_key_symbol :syntax/special
   :delimited_symbol :syntax/special
   :integer :syntax/number
   :float :syntax/number
   :constant :syntax/special
   :instance_variable :syntax/function
   :global_variable :syntax/function
   :class_variable :syntax/function
   ;; a call's method (`foo(x)`, `s.bar`, `puts`) and a definition's name: Ruby has no
   ;; field access, so every `x.y` IS a call and colouring its method never lies
   {:parent :call :field :method :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:parent :method :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :singleton_method :field :name} :syntax/function})

;; ---- ruby's fontify restart ---------------------------------------------------
;; Elixir decides restart safety by counting PAIRED heredoc markers; Ruby has no pair to
;; count — `<<~EOS` ends at a line holding whatever identifier the opener named. So Ruby
;; asks a weaker question with a safe answer: is there any heredoc opener, or any `=begin`
;; block comment, above the candidate at all? If so it declines and keeps the full lead-in,
;; which is exactly today's behaviour. Over-declining costs speed in files that open a
;; heredoc near the viewport and nothing else; guessing would colour heredoc prose as code.
;;
;; Bare `<<EOS` is recognised by the uppercase/underscore that conventionally follows,
;; because `<<` alone is `Array#<<` and left-shift — searching for it would decline
;; everywhere and buy nothing.

(defn- ruby-upper? (c)
  ;; an uppercase ASCII letter, tested by case-folding (Brood's `<` is numeric-only)
  (and (not (= (string/upper c) (string/lower c))) (= c (string/upper c))))

(sig ruby-heredoc-open-at? (string int -> bool))
(defn- ruby-heredoc-open-at? (src i)
  ;; `i` is the offset of a `<<` — does it open a heredoc rather than shift or append?
  (let (j (+ i 2))
    (and (< j (string/length src))
      (let (c (string/char-at src j))
        (or (= c "~") (= c "-") (= c "'") (= c "\"") (= c "_") (ruby-upper? c))))))

(defn- ruby-heredoc-above? (src upto from)
  ;; any heredoc opener in `src` before `upto`
  (let (i (index-of src "<<" from))
    (cond
      (or (< i 0) (>= i upto)) false
      (ruby-heredoc-open-at? src i) true
      else (ruby-heredoc-above? src upto (+ i 2)))))

(defn- ruby-block-comment-above? (src upto)
  ;; `=begin` / `=end` are column-0 only, so a newline-prefixed match (or offset 0) is one
  (or (string/starts-with? src "=begin")
    (let (i (index-of src "\n=begin")) (and (>= i 0) (< i upto)))))

(defn ruby-fontify-restart (src pos)
  "Ruby's `:fontify-restart`: the previous column-0 line, unless a heredoc or `=begin` block
opens above it in this window — in which case keep the whole lead-in, as before."
  (let (candidate (editor/treesit/column0-before src pos))
    (if (or (ruby-heredoc-above? src candidate 0) (ruby-block-comment-above? src candidate))
      0
      candidate)))

(deflanguage ruby
  {:lang :ruby
   :pattern "\\.rb$"
   :faces languages/ruby-face-table
   :indent languages/ruby-indent-spec
   ;; its own: a heredoc terminator is arbitrary text, and `=begin` blocks count too
   :restart 'languages/ruby-fontify-restart
   ;; the community standard, and what a Ruby project has if it has anything
   :format ["rubocop" "-a" "--stderr" "--format" "quiet"]
   :comment "# "})

;; Elixir: def/defmodule/if/case are macros, parsed as calls, so only their TEXT tells
;; them from `foo(x)`: a call target named one of `elixir-keyword-calls` takes the keyword
;; colour (brood ADR-487), as `do`/`end`/`fn`/`when`/… do. Atoms and module
;; aliases read as `:special`. A call's name — the identifier in its `target` field —
;; is a function: `foo(x)`, and the name a `def` defines (`def main(x)` is a call inside a
;; call).
;; `Enum.map`'s `map` is left alone: a `dot`'s right side is also a map's field (`x.field`),
;; and nothing in the node's context tells the two apart.
(def- elixir-keyword-calls
  #{"def"
    "defp" "defmodule" "defmacro" "defmacrop" "defstruct" "defprotocol" "defimpl" "defdelegate"
    "defguard" "defguardp" "defexception" "defoverridable" "if" "unless" "case" "cond" "with" "for"
    "receive" "try" "quote" "unquote" "use" "import" "alias" "require"})

(def elixir-face-table
  {{:parent :call :field :target :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:parent :call :field :target :kind :identifier :text elixir-keyword-calls} :syntax/keyword
   :comment :syntax/comment
   :string :syntax/string
   :charlist :syntax/string
   :sigil :syntax/string
   :integer :syntax/number
   :float :syntax/number
   :atom :syntax/special
   :alias :syntax/special
   :keyword :syntax/special})

;; Elixir's paired multi-line string markers — what the restart counts to decide whether a
;; column-0 line is real code or prose inside an `@moduledoc`.
(def *elixir-heredocs* ["\"\"\"" "'''"])

;; ---- HEEx: Phoenix templates, in `.heex` files and inside Elixir's `~H` ------------------
;; A modest table: tags, components and slots by name, attributes, strings, comments, and
;; the delimiters of embedded Elixir (`{…}`, `<%= … %>`) — the Elixir inside stays plain,
;; since injections do not nest (brood ADR-489).
(def heex-face-table
  {:tag_name :syntax/keyword
   :component_name :syntax/function
   :slot_name :syntax/function
   :attribute_name :syntax/special
   :quoted_attribute_value :syntax/string
   :comment :syntax/comment
   {:parent :expression :kind (keyword "{")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :expression :kind (keyword "}")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :directive :kind (keyword "<%")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :directive :kind (keyword "<%=")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :directive :kind (keyword "%>")} :syntax/special})

;; a tag, component or slot is one level, and a line opening with `</` backs out of it, as
;; JSX's does (`js-indent-spec`) — only `)]}` close by themselves
(def heex-indent-spec
  {:width 2 :blocks #{} :brackets #{:tag :component :slot} :dedent #{"</"} :opens #{}})

(deflanguage heex
  {:lang :heex
   :pattern "\\.heex$"
   :faces languages/heex-face-table
   :indent languages/heex-indent-spec
   :grammar "https://github.com/phoenixframework/tree-sitter-heex"})

;; `~H"""…"""` in an Elixir file is HEEx: the sigil's content, named by its sigil letter
(def elixir-injections
  [{:content {:kind :quoted_content :parent :sigil} :lang-from :sigil_name :aliases {"H" :heex}}])

(deflanguage elixir
  {:lang :elixir
   :pattern "\\.(ex|exs)$"
   :faces languages/elixir-face-table
   :indent languages/elixir-indent-spec
   :heredocs languages/*elixir-heredocs*
   :injections languages/elixir-injections
   :format ["mix" "format"]
   :comment "# "})

;; ---- python-mode: a language the runtime was never built with ---------------
;; The proof that a language is a data entry rather than a build: nothing in Brood knows
;; Python. Drop `libtree-sitter-python.so` into `~/.config/bedit/grammars/` and everything
;; below — colouring, the `C-M-*` structural motions, indentation — comes from these three
;; definitions. Without the grammar the mode still registers and simply cannot parse.
;;
;; Python indents by four, and every level is the same `:block` node, so it needs no dedent
;; set at all: `else:` sits in the `if`'s own block chain, not inside the arm it follows.
(def python-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :string :syntax/string
   :string_content :syntax/string
   :integer :syntax/number
   :float :syntax/number
   :true :syntax/special
   :false :syntax/special
   :none :syntax/special
   ;; a called name (`print(x)`), a method call's (`s.bar()`: the attribute of an
   ;; `attribute` that is a call's function, brood ADR-487 — `p.x` stays uncoloured) and a
   ;; defined one
   {:field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:kind :identifier :field :attribute :parent {:kind :attribute :field :function}} :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_definition :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :class_definition :field :name} :syntax/special})

(def python-indent-spec
  {:width 4
   :blocks #{:block} ; every suite is a `block`; `else:` sits in the `if`'s own chain
   ;; the bracketed literals and call arguments — a multi-line one indents (or aligns)
   ;; its contents, and must not indent its own opening line
   :brackets #{:list :dictionary :set :tuple :argument_list :parameters :parenthesized_expression}
   ;; Nothing to declare: `else:`/`elif:`/`except:` are children of the statement rather
   ;; than of its block, so they already measure at the outer level, and the closing
   ;; delimiters decide for themselves.
   :dedent #{}})

(deflanguage python
  {:lang :python
   :pattern "\\.py$"
   :faces languages/python-face-table
   :indent languages/python-indent-spec
   ;; paired triple quotes, exactly Elixir's case
   :heredocs languages/*elixir-heredocs*
   :format ["black" "-q"]
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-python"
   :definitions {:kinds #{:function_definition :class_definition}}
   :comment "# "})

;; ---- rust-mode: Python's case, a grammar loaded at runtime --------------------
;; `~/.config/bedit/grammars/libtree-sitter-rust.so`. The LSP (rust-analyzer), the
;; toolchain row (cargo) and the `-->` error locations were already here; this is the mode
;; they were waiting for.
;;
;; Every Rust level is a `{`-delimited node, so they are all `:brackets` rather than
;; `:blocks`: each node SPANS its own `}`, and a closing line measured inside it must back
;; out the bracket it closes — which a delimiter decides for itself, and a block would not.
;; As brackets they also collapse per line, so `xs.map(|x| {` opens one level, not two.
;; No keyword dedents: `} else {` begins with the delimiter.
(def rust-face-table
  {:line_comment :syntax/comment
   :block_comment :syntax/comment
   :string_literal :syntax/string
   :raw_string_literal :syntax/string
   :char_literal :syntax/string
   :integer_literal :syntax/number
   :float_literal :syntax/number
   :boolean_literal :syntax/special
   :lifetime :syntax/special
   :type_identifier :syntax/special
   :primitive_type :syntax/special
   :attribute_item :syntax/special
   :inner_attribute_item :syntax/special
   ;; named nodes, so the anonymous-keyword rule never sees them
   :self :syntax/keyword
   :crate :syntax/keyword
   :super :syntax/keyword
   :mutable_specifier :syntax/keyword
   ;; a called function, a macro, and the name a `fn` defines (brood ADR-486); a method
   ;; call (`s.bar()`) and a path call (`a::b()`) by their grandparent, the call (ADR-487),
   ;; so a plain field read (`p.x`) and a type path stay uncoloured
   {:field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:kind :field_identifier :field :field :parent {:kind :field_expression :field :function}} :syntax/function
   {:kind :identifier :field :name :parent {:kind :scoped_identifier :field :function}} :syntax/function
   {:parent :macro_invocation :field :macro} :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_item :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_signature_item :field :name} :syntax/function})

(def rust-indent-spec
  {:width 4
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:block
               :declaration_list :field_declaration_list :enum_variant_list :match_block
               :field_initializer_list :use_list :token_tree :arguments
               :parameters :array_expression :tuple_expression :ordered_field_declaration_list
               :struct_pattern :tuple_pattern :tuple_struct_pattern :parenthesized_expression}
   :dedent #{}})

;; ---- rust's fontify restart ---------------------------------------------------
;; A column-0 line is not necessarily code: a plain Rust string may span lines, and
;; `const USAGE: &str = "usage:\nbedit FILE\n";` puts its prose in column 0. So the candidate
;; must also LOOK like the start of an item — an item keyword, an attribute, a comment or a
;; closing brace — and an earlier column-0 line is tried when it does not. A raw string or a
;; block comment opened above has no such tell (its contents are anything, items included),
;; so either declines the cut, as Ruby's heredocs do.

(def rust-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^((pub|fn|impl|struct|enum|union|trait|mod|use|const|static|type|unsafe|async|extern)\\b|macro_rules!|#!?\\[|//|\\})"
   :opaque "/\\*|(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])b?r#*\""})

(defn- item-line-before (src pos item-start)
  ;; the nearest column-0 line at or before `pos` that matches `item-start`, or 0
  (let (candidate (editor/treesit/column0-before src pos)
        eol (let (i (index-of src "\n" candidate)) (if (< i 0) (string/length src) i)))
    (cond
      (= candidate 0) 0
      (regex/match? item-start (string/substring src candidate eol)) candidate
      else (item-line-before src (dec candidate) item-start))))

(defn column0-item-restart (src pos item-start opaque-opener)
  "The fontify restart every brace language shares: the previous column-0 line that
LOOKS like the start of an item (`item-start`, a regex over the line), unless something
whose contents can be anything — a block comment, a raw or template string
(`opaque-opener`) — opens above it in this window; then the whole lead-in. A language with
no such thing passes nil."
  (let (candidate (item-line-before src pos item-start)
        opener (when opaque-opener (regex/find opaque-opener src)))
    (if (and opener (< (get opener :start) candidate)) 0 candidate)))

(deflanguage rust
  {:lang :rust
   :pattern "\\.rs$"
   :faces languages/rust-face-table
   :indent languages/rust-indent-spec
   :restart languages/rust-restart-rule
   ;; rustfmt alone formats as the 2015 edition (cargo fmt passes the crate's); a new
   ;; crate is 2024
   :format ["rustfmt" "--edition" "2024"]
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-rust"
   ;; Emacs rust-ts-mode's defun types: a `fn` inside an `impl` is its own definition
   :definitions {:kinds #{:function_item
                          :impl_item :trait_item :struct_item :enum_item :union_item :type_item
                          :mod_item :macro_definition}}
   :comment "// "})

;; ---- the brace languages: JavaScript, TypeScript, TSX, Go, C ------------------------
;; Rust's shape four more times: a face table, the `{`-delimited nodes as `:brackets`, the
;; definitions C-M-a stops at (Emacs's *-ts-mode defun types), and the column-0 restart.
;; Each names where its grammar lives, so `M-x grammar-install` can fetch it.
;;
;; A matched node is painted whole (`editor/treesit/fontify` stops there), so a template
;; string colours its `string_fragment`s rather than itself — what is inside `${…}` stays
;; code.

(def js-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :hash_bang_line :syntax/comment
   :string :syntax/string
   :string_fragment :syntax/string
   :escape_sequence :syntax/string
   :regex :syntax/string
   :number :syntax/number
   :true :syntax/special
   :false :syntax/special
   :null :syntax/special
   :undefined :syntax/special
   :decorator :syntax/special
   ;; named nodes, so the anonymous-keyword rule never sees them
   :this :syntax/keyword
   :super :syntax/keyword
   ;; a called function and a defined one (brood ADR-486), and a method call's name —
   ;; `console.log`'s `log`, told from a plain property read by its grandparent (ADR-487)
   {:field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:kind :property_identifier
    :field :property
    :parent {:kind :member_expression :field :function}} :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :generator_function_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :method_definition :field :name} :syntax/function})

(def ts-face-table
  (merge js-face-table
    {:type_identifier :syntax/special
     :predefined_type :syntax/special
     :literal_type :syntax/special}))

(def js-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   ;; a `case`'s statements sit one deeper than the `case`, which sits one deeper than
   ;; the `switch` — the body is a bracket, and so is each case
   :brackets #{:statement_block
               :class_body :object :array :arguments :formal_parameters :switch_body
               :switch_case :switch_default :object_pattern :array_pattern
               :parenthesized_expression :named_imports :export_clause :template_substitution
               :jsx_element :jsx_opening_element :jsx_self_closing_element}
   ;; a JSX closing tag leaves its element the way `}` leaves a block
   :dedent #{"</"}})

(def ts-indent-spec
  (assoc js-indent-spec
    :brackets
      (into (get js-indent-spec :brackets)
        #{:interface_body :enum_body :object_type :type_arguments :type_parameters})))

(def js-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^((export|import|function|class|const|let|var|async|interface|type|enum|declare|abstract|namespace|module)\\b|//|@|\\})"
   ;; a block comment or a template string — either may hold anything, column 0 included
   :opaque "/\\*|`"})

(def js-definitions
  {:kinds #{:function_declaration
            :generator_function_declaration :class_declaration
            :method_definition :lexical_declaration}})

(def ts-definitions
  {:kinds (into (get js-definitions :kinds)
            #{:interface_declaration
              :type_alias_declaration :enum_declaration :abstract_class_declaration :module})})

(deflanguage javascript
  {:lang :javascript
   :pattern "\\.(js|mjs|cjs|jsx)$"
   :faces languages/js-face-table
   :indent languages/js-indent-spec
   :restart languages/js-restart-rule
   :format ["prettier" "--write" "--log-level" "warn"]
   :definitions languages/js-definitions
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-javascript"
   :comment "// "})

;; TypeScript is JavaScript's spec with its own grammar, faces, indent and definitions; TSX is
;; TypeScript's with the JSX grammar — the formatter, restart rule and comment come along
(deflanguage typescript
  (merge languages/javascript-spec
    {:lang :typescript
     :pattern "\\.(ts|mts|cts)$"
     :faces languages/ts-face-table
     :indent languages/ts-indent-spec
     :definitions languages/ts-definitions
     :grammar {:url "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-typescript" :subdir "typescript"}}))

(deflanguage tsx
  (merge languages/typescript-spec
    {:lang :tsx
     :pattern "\\.tsx$"
     :grammar {:url "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-typescript" :subdir "tsx"}}))

(def go-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :interpreted_string_literal :syntax/string
   :raw_string_literal :syntax/string
   :rune_literal :syntax/string
   :int_literal :syntax/number
   :float_literal :syntax/number
   :imaginary_literal :syntax/number
   :true :syntax/special
   :false :syntax/special
   :nil :syntax/special
   :iota :syntax/special
   :type_identifier :syntax/special
   ;; a called function and a defined one or method (brood ADR-486), and a selector call's
   ;; name — `fmt.Println`'s `Println`, told from `p.X` by its grandparent (ADR-487)
   {:field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:kind :field_identifier
    :field :field
    :parent {:kind :selector_expression :field :function}} :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :method_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function})

;; gofmt's layout: tabs, and a `case` at the `switch`'s own level with its statements one
;; deeper. A switch carries its braces itself (there is no body node), so the switch IS the
;; bracket — its statements one deeper — and a `case` / `default` line backs out of it.
(def go-indent-spec
  {:width 8
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:block
               :field_declaration_list :interface_type :literal_value :argument_list
               :parameter_list :import_spec_list :var_spec_list :const_declaration
               :type_declaration :parenthesized_expression
               :expression_switch_statement :type_switch_statement :select_statement}
   :dedent #{"case" "default"}})

(def go-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^((func|type|var|const|import|package)\\b|//|\\})"
   ;; a block comment or a raw string — `…` spans lines
   :opaque "/\\*|`"})

(deflanguage go
  {:lang :go
   :pattern "\\.go$"
   :faces languages/go-face-table
   :indent languages/go-indent-spec
   :indent-tabs true
   :restart languages/go-restart-rule
   :format ["gofmt" "-w"]
   :definitions {:kinds #{:function_declaration :method_declaration :type_declaration}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-go"
   :comment "// "})

;; go.mod (camdencheek/tree-sitter-go-mod, whose grammar is named `gomod`): the directive
;; words are anonymous tokens, so the keyword rule colours them; a module path and a
;; replacement's file path paint as strings, a version as a number — the two things a line
;; of go.mod says. A `require ( … )` block is one level, tab-indented as `go mod tidy` writes
;; it; a one-line directive opens nothing a later line sits in.
(def gomod-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :module_path :syntax/string
   :file_path :syntax/string
   :version :syntax/number
   :go_version :syntax/number
   :toolchain_name :syntax/number
   (keyword "=>") :syntax/special})

(def gomod-directives
  #{:require_directive
    :replace_directive :exclude_directive :retract_directive :module_directive
    :go_directive :toolchain_directive :godebug_directive :tool_directive :ignore_directive})

(deflanguage gomod
  {:lang :gomod
   :pattern "(^|/)go\\.mod$"
   :faces languages/gomod-face-table
   ;; Go's width: a level is one tab, which the indenter counts as eight columns
   :indent {:width 8
            :blocks #{}
            :brackets #{:require_directive
                        :replace_directive :exclude_directive :retract_directive :godebug_directive
                        :tool_directive :ignore_directive}
            :dedent #{}
            :opens #{"("}}
   :indent-tabs true
   ;; every directive starts at column 0 and nothing spans lines but a `( … )` block
   :restart {:item-start "^([a-z]|//|\\))" :opaque nil}
   ;; `go mod edit -fmt` takes the file to format as its argument
   :format ["go" "mod" "edit" "-fmt"]
   :definitions {:kinds languages/gomod-directives}
   :grammar "https://github.com/camdencheek/tree-sitter-go-mod"
   :comment "// "})

(def c-face-table
  (merge
    {:comment :syntax/comment
     :string_literal :syntax/string
     :system_lib_string :syntax/string
     :char_literal :syntax/string
     :number_literal :syntax/number
     :true :syntax/special
     :false :syntax/special
     :null :syntax/special
     :primitive_type :syntax/special
     :type_identifier :syntax/special
     :preproc_directive :syntax/keyword
     ;; a called function, and the name a function declarator declares (brood
     ;; ADR-486) — `area` in `static int area(…)`, a prototype's too
     {:field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
     ;; a call through a struct's function pointer (`ops->open(f)`), not a field read (ADR-487)
     {:kind :field_identifier :field :field :parent {:kind :field_expression :field :function}} :syntax/function
     {:parent :function_declarator :field :declarator :kind :identifier} :syntax/function}
    ; a directive the grammar does not know
    ;; `#include`, `#define`, … are ANONYMOUS tokens beginning with `#`, which the keyword
    ;; rule (a token beginning with a letter) cannot see — so they are named here by kind
    (into {}
      (map ["#include"
             "#define" "#undef" "#if" "#ifdef" "#ifndef" "#elif" "#elifdef" "#elifndef" "#else"
             "#endif"]
        (fn (directive) [(keyword directive) :syntax/keyword])))))

;; Four columns (K&R and most C; clang-format's LLVM default is two), and a `case` at the
;; `switch`'s level with its statements one deeper — the switch body is a block like any
;; other, and `case` / `default` back out of it.
(def c-indent-spec
  {:width 4
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:compound_statement
               :field_declaration_list :enumerator_list :initializer_list :argument_list
               :parameter_list :parenthesized_expression}
   :dedent #{"case" "default"}})

;; a column-0 line in C that is not code is inside a block comment; a declaration starts
;; with a word, a preprocessor line with `#`
(def c-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^([A-Za-z_#]|//|\\})"
   :opaque "/\\*"})

(deflanguage c
  {:lang :c
   :pattern "\\.[ch]$"
   :faces languages/c-face-table
   :indent languages/c-indent-spec
   :restart languages/c-restart-rule
   :format ["clang-format" "-i"]
   :definitions {:kinds #{:function_definition :struct_specifier :enum_specifier :union_specifier}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-c"
   :comment "// "})

;; ---- HCL and Terraform: one grammar, two modes ----------------------------------------
;; tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-hcl builds the grammar twice — the root and
;; `dialects/terraform` — from ONE `make_grammar.js`, differing only in the name the library
;; exports. So one `:hcl` grammar parses `.tf`, `.tfvars` and `.hcl` alike, and the two
;; modes below share it, told apart by `:type`: Terraform files format with `terraform fmt`,
;; which refuses any other extension, and a plain `.hcl` file (Packer, Nomad, Vault
;; config) has no formatter here.
;;
;; Every name in HCL is an `identifier` — a block's type (`resource`), an attribute's name
;; and a function's name alike — so the three are told apart by where the identifier sits:
;; context selectors (brood ADR-486). A block's type is its FIRST named child (its labels
;; follow, as `string_lit`s); an attribute's name and a call's function are the identifiers
;; directly under an `attribute` and a `function_call`. A traversal (`var.environment`,
;; `aws_s3_bucket.logs.arn`) stays uncoloured: it is the code, as inside `${…}`.
(def hcl-face-table
  {{:parent :block :kind :identifier :nth 0} :syntax/keyword
   {:parent :attribute :kind :identifier} :syntax/special
   {:parent :function_call :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   :comment :syntax/comment
   ;; a block label, or a string with no template in it — painted whole
   :string_lit :syntax/string
   ;; a template string's own text and quotes; what is inside `${…}` stays code
   :template_literal :syntax/string
   :quoted_template_start :syntax/string
   :quoted_template_end :syntax/string
   :heredoc_start :syntax/string
   :heredoc_identifier :syntax/string
   :template_interpolation_start :syntax/special
   :template_interpolation_end :syntax/special
   :template_directive_start :syntax/special
   :template_directive_end :syntax/special
   :numeric_lit :syntax/number
   :bool_lit :syntax/special
   :null_lit :syntax/special})

;; `terraform fmt`'s layout: two spaces, one level per block, object, tuple and argument
;; list. Every one is a `{` / `[` / `(` node that spans its own closer, so they are all
;; `:brackets` — and a block is one too, though it begins at its type rather than at `{`:
;; the opening line is the block's own, and its closing `}` backs out by itself.
(def hcl-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:block :object :tuple :function_call :for_tuple_expr :for_object_expr}
   :dedent #{}
   ;; while a block is being typed nothing parses, and a line ending in an opener is the
   ;; one hint left that the next line goes a level deeper
   :opens #{"{" "[" "("}
   ;; a heredoc's body and its closing marker are text: TAB on one of their lines keeps
   ;; the indentation it has (brood ADR-486) — a `<<-` body is the writer's layout
   :verbatim #{:heredoc_template}})

;; a block or an attribute starts at column 0; a heredoc's body or a block comment may hold
;; anything there
(def hcl-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^([A-Za-z_]|#|//|\\})"
   :opaque "/\\*|<<"})

;; every block is a definition — `resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" { … }`, and a nested
;; `lifecycle { … }` is the deepest one, as a Rust `fn` inside an `impl` is
(def hcl-definitions {:kinds #{:block}})

;; a heredoc whose marker names a language is that language's code (brood ADR-489): a
;; `user_data = <<-BASH` script is shell, coloured with shell-mode's table, its pieces
;; between `${…}` interpolations ONE document. The marker is the heredoc's
;; `heredoc_identifier`, a sibling of its `template_literal` pieces; a marker naming no
;; language (`EOT`) keeps the string colour
(def hcl-injections
  [{:content {:kind :template_literal :parent :heredoc_template}
    :lang-from :heredoc_identifier
    :aliases {"BASH" :bash
              "SH" :bash
              "SHELL" :bash
              "JSON" :json
              "YAML" :yaml
              "PY" :python
              "PYTHON" :python}}])

;; registered first: the most recent registration wins a file name, and a `.tftest.hcl` file
;; is Terraform's
(deflanguage hcl
  {:lang :hcl
   :pattern "\\.hcl$"
   :faces languages/hcl-face-table
   :indent languages/hcl-indent-spec
   :restart languages/hcl-restart-rule
   :definitions languages/hcl-definitions
   :injections languages/hcl-injections
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-hcl"
   :comment "# "})

(deflanguage terraform
  (merge languages/hcl-spec
    {:type :terraform
     ;; `.tftest.hcl` is a Terraform test file; OpenTofu adds `.tofu` and `.tofutest.hcl`
     :pattern "\\.(tf|tfvars|tofu)$|\\.(tf|tofu)test\\.hcl$"
     ;; HashiCorp's, else OpenTofu's — the same layout from either
     :format [["terraform" "fmt"] ["tofu" "fmt"]]}))

;; ---- the lexed languages, parsed once their grammar is installed ---------------------
;; Shell, JSON, TOML, YAML and Dockerfile were line-lexer modes before they had grammars,
;; and each keeps its lexer as the `:fallback`: a buffer opened while the grammar is missing
;; is coloured line by line as before (`model/ed-grammar-fallback`), and `M-x
;; grammar-install` turns it into the parsed mode in place. What the grammar adds is what a
;; line cannot know — where a multi-line string or a heredoc ends, which block a line is in
;; (indentation, C-M-a / C-M-e) and which bracket a bracket closes.

;; sh / bash / zsh: by extension, by name (`.bashrc`), OR by the `#!` line — so `run`,
;; `deploy`, `configure` with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` on top get the mode with no extension.
;; tree-sitter-bash parses all three well enough to colour and indent.
;;
;; A double-quoted string colours its text and quotes, not itself: what is inside `$x`,
;; `${…}` and `$(…)` is code, as in a JavaScript template. A heredoc's body is painted
;; whole — it is text. Every command's name is a call (`command_name`), which the lexer could
;; only guess at with a list of builtins.
(def shell-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :string_content :syntax/string
   (keyword "\"") :syntax/string
   :raw_string :syntax/string
   :ansi_c_string :syntax/string
   :translated_string :syntax/string
   :heredoc_start :syntax/string
   :heredoc_body :syntax/string
   :heredoc_end :syntax/string
   :regex :syntax/string
   :number :syntax/number
   :file_descriptor :syntax/number
   :simple_expansion :syntax/special
   :expansion :syntax/special
   {:parent :command_substitution :kind (keyword "$(")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :command_substitution :kind (keyword ")")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :command_substitution :kind (keyword "`")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :arithmetic_expansion :kind (keyword "$((")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :arithmetic_expansion :kind (keyword "))")} :syntax/special
   {:parent :variable_assignment :kind :variable_name} :syntax/special
   :test_operator :syntax/special
   :command_name :syntax/function
   {:parent :function_definition :kind :word} :syntax/function})

;; Every level is a construct that opened on an earlier line — `{ … }`, `do … done`, an
;; `if` (whose statements are its own children: there is no body node), a `case` and each
;; of its arms — so they are all `:brackets`, which count only for the lines after the one
;; they open on and collapse per line (`foo | while read x; do` opens one level, not
;; three). A command, pipeline or `&&` list continued with `\` indents its continuation
;; lines one level, as shfmt does. `elif` / `else` / `fi` / `esac` / `done` back out of
;; what they close; a `then` or `do` on its own line sits at its statement's level.
(def shell-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:compound_statement
               :subshell :do_group :if_statement :case_statement :case_item :array
               :command_substitution :command :pipeline :list
               :redirected_statement :variable_assignment :declaration_command :test_command}
   :dedent #{"then" "elif" "else" "fi" "done" "esac"}
   ;; while a block is being typed nothing parses, and a line ending in an opener is the
   ;; one hint left that the next line goes a level deeper
   :opens #{"{" "(" "then" "do" "else" "in"}
   ;; a heredoc and a multi-line string are content: TAB on one of their lines keeps the
   ;; indentation it has, and the end marker sits where it must
   :verbatim #{:heredoc_redirect :string :raw_string :ansi_c_string}})

;; A column-0 line inside a multi-line string or a heredoc is prose, so the candidate must
;; LOOK like a statement a script starts at the margin — an assignment, a definition, a
;; control keyword, a comment, a closing brace — and a heredoc opened above declines.
(def shell-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_:.-]*[ ]*\\(\\)|(function|if|for|while|until|case|select|export|readonly|declare|local|set|source|trap)\\b|#|\\})"
   :opaque "<<"})

(deflanguage shell
  {:lang :bash
   :type :shell
   :pattern "[.](sh|bash|zsh|ksh|dash)$|(^|/)[.]?(bashrc|bash_profile|bash_aliases|bash_login|bash_logout|zshrc|zshenv|zprofile|zlogin|profile|xprofile|xinitrc)$|(^|/)PKGBUILD$"
   :interpreters ["sh" "bash" "zsh" "ksh" "dash" "ash" "busybox"]
   :faces languages/shell-face-table
   :indent languages/shell-indent-spec
   :restart languages/shell-restart-rule
   :format ["shfmt" "-w"]
   :definitions {:kinds #{:function_definition}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-bash"
   :fallback 'lex-shell/shell
   :comment "# "})

;; JSON (and JSONC: tsconfig, VS Code settings): tree-sitter-json reads `//` and `/* */`
;; comments too. A key paints as a keyword, as the lexer painted it, so an object's shape
;; reads at a glance; a value string as a string, the three literals as specials.
(def json-face-table
  {{:parent :pair :field :key} :syntax/keyword
   :string :syntax/string
   :number :syntax/number
   :true :syntax/special
   :false :syntax/special
   :null :syntax/special
   :comment :syntax/comment})

(def json-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:object :array}
   :dedent #{}
   :opens #{"{" "["}})

;; A JSON file past a megabyte is the lexer's, grammar or not. Such a file is written by a
;; program (an API dump, a lock file, a bundle's source map), read rather than edited, and
;; often one line long. A held tree has a node every few characters — several bytes of tree
;; per byte of text, held for as long as the buffer is open — and every edit past
;; `syntaxtree/*tree-sync-max*` reparses off the loop. What the grammar adds over the lexer
;; is indentation and structural motion, which is what a hand-edited file wants, and a
;; hand-edited JSON file is far below this. The lexer pays for the lines on screen only.
(def json-lexer-above 1000000)

(deflanguage json
  {:lang :json
   ;; JSON5 is not JSON and stays lexed, and JSON Lines has its own mode (both below)
   :pattern "\\.(json|jsonc|webmanifest)$|(^|/)\\.(babelrc|eslintrc|prettierrc)$"
   :faces languages/json-face-table
   :indent languages/json-indent-spec
   ;; a column-0 line is a bracket, unless a JSONC block comment opened above
   :restart {:item-start "^[\\[\\]{}]" :opaque "/\\*"}
   :format ["prettier" "--write" "--log-level" "warn"]
   ;; json-ts-mode's: C-M-a goes to the start of the key/value pair point is in
   :definitions {:kinds #{:pair}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-json"
   :fallback 'lex-configs/json
   :fallback-above languages/json-lexer-above
   :comment "// "})

;; JSON Lines: a value per line, which the grammar's document (any number of values) parses
;; as it stands — JSON's mode over the same grammar, told apart by `:type` as Terraform is
;; from HCL, without the formatter: prettier infers no parser for `.jsonl` and would fail
;; every save
(deflanguage jsonl
  (merge (dissoc languages/json-spec :format) {:type :jsonl :pattern "\\.jsonl$"}))

;; JSON5's unquoted keys and trailing commas are errors to tree-sitter-json; its lexer reads
;; them as JSON's
(deflexer json5
  {:pattern "\\.json5$"
   :lexer 'lex-configs/json
   :comment "// "})

;; TOML (tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-toml): a table's header — its brackets and its
;; name — is a function, as the lexer painted it, and a key a keyword; a dotted key is
;; painted whole. A table's pairs are its children, so a table is a DEFINITION: C-M-a goes
;; to the `[header]` point is under. Entries are not indented under a header (taplo's
;; default): only an array or an inline table spanning lines opens a level, and a
;; multi-line string is content.
(def toml-face-table
  {{:parent :table :kind :bare_key} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table :kind :dotted_key} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table :kind :quoted_key} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table :kind (keyword "[")} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table :kind (keyword "]")} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table_array_element :kind :bare_key} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table_array_element :kind :dotted_key} :syntax/function
   {:parent :table_array_element :kind :quoted_key} :syntax/function
   (keyword "[[") :syntax/function
   (keyword "]]") :syntax/function
   {:parent :pair :kind :bare_key} :syntax/keyword
   {:parent :pair :kind :dotted_key} :syntax/keyword
   {:parent :pair :kind :quoted_key} :syntax/keyword
   :string :syntax/string
   :integer :syntax/number
   :float :syntax/number
   :offset_date_time :syntax/number
   :local_date_time :syntax/number
   :local_date :syntax/number
   :local_time :syntax/number
   :boolean :syntax/special
   :comment :syntax/comment})

(def toml-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:array :inline_table}
   :dedent #{}
   :opens #{"[" "{"}
   :verbatim #{:string}})

(deflanguage toml
  {:lang :toml
   :pattern "\\.toml$|(^|/)(Cargo|Pipfile|poetry)\\.lock$"
   :faces languages/toml-face-table
   :indent languages/toml-indent-spec
   ;; paired triple quotes, Python's case: a column-0 line inside one is text
   :heredocs ["\"\"\"" "'''"]
   :format ["taplo" "format"]
   :definitions {:kinds #{:table :table_array_element}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-toml"
   :fallback 'lex-configs/toml
   :comment "# "})

;; YAML (tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-yaml): a key — block or flow, plain or quoted —
;; is a keyword, painted whole, as the lexer painted it; quoted and block scalars are
;; strings to their last line (the lexer coloured a `|` block's lines not at all), YAML 1.2's
;; numbers, booleans and null by the scalar's kind, anchors, aliases, tags and the
;; document markers specials. A plain scalar value stays uncoloured: it is the data.
(def yaml-face-table
  {{:parent :block_mapping_pair :field :key} :syntax/keyword
   {:parent :flow_pair :field :key} :syntax/keyword
   :double_quote_scalar :syntax/string
   :single_quote_scalar :syntax/string
   :block_scalar :syntax/string
   :integer_scalar :syntax/number
   :float_scalar :syntax/number
   :boolean_scalar :syntax/special
   :null_scalar :syntax/special
   :anchor :syntax/special
   :alias :syntax/special
   :tag :syntax/special
   :yaml_directive :syntax/special
   :tag_directive :syntax/special
   :reserved_directive :syntax/special
   (keyword "---") :syntax/special
   (keyword "...") :syntax/special
   :comment :syntax/comment})

;; YAML's indentation IS its structure: the tree is built from the columns, so it cannot
;; say where a line belongs — a line moved two columns left is a different document, which
;; parses. No spec counts levels here: every line is CONTENT (`:indent :content`,
;; [[content-indent]]), so TAB leaves a line where it is (text-mode's TAB re-indents it to
;; the line above, which rewrites the data) and RET carries the previous line's indentation.
;; The first content line included — a `:verbatim #{:stream}` spec missed it, the stream
;; node beginning on that very line, and TAB moved an indented first line to column 0.

(deflanguage yaml
  {:lang :yaml
   :pattern "\\.(ya?ml)$|(^|/)\\.(clang-format|yamllint)$"
   :faces languages/yaml-face-table
   :indent :content
   ;; a top-level key, a list item, a comment or a document marker; a block scalar's lines
   ;; are indented, so there is nothing whose contents reach column 0 to decline for
   :restart {:item-start "^([A-Za-z0-9_\"'-]|#|---|\\.\\.\\.)" :opaque nil}
   :format ["prettier" "--write" "--log-level" "warn"]
   ;; C-M-a to the key point is under, the deepest one
   :definitions {:kinds #{:block_mapping_pair}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter-grammars/tree-sitter-yaml"
   :fallback 'lex-configs/yaml
   :comment "# "})

;; Dockerfile (camdencheek/tree-sitter-dockerfile): `Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.dev`,
;; `*.dockerfile`, and Podman's `Containerfile`. The instruction words (FROM, RUN, AS) are
;; the grammar's anonymous tokens, so the keyword rule colours them in any case the
;; grammar accepts; strings, a heredoc's body and an `EXPOSE` port by node; an image's name
;; is a function and its tag, an expansion, a `--flag=` and a variable's name specials.
(def docker-instructions
  #{:from_instruction
    :run_instruction :cmd_instruction :label_instruction :maintainer_instruction
    :expose_instruction :env_instruction :add_instruction :copy_instruction
    :entrypoint_instruction :volume_instruction :user_instruction :workdir_instruction
    :arg_instruction :onbuild_instruction :stopsignal_instruction :healthcheck_instruction
    :shell_instruction :cross_build_instruction})

(def docker-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :double_quoted_string :syntax/string
   :single_quoted_string :syntax/string
   :json_string :syntax/string
   :heredoc_marker :syntax/string
   :heredoc_block :syntax/string
   :expose_port :syntax/number
   :image_name :syntax/function
   :image_alias :syntax/function
   :image_tag :syntax/special
   :image_digest :syntax/special
   :expansion :syntax/special
   :param :syntax/special
   :mount_param :syntax/special
   {:parent :env_pair :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :label_pair :field :key} :syntax/special
   {:parent :arg_instruction :field :name} :syntax/special})

;; An instruction continued with `\` indents its continuation lines one level — four
;; columns, as Docker's own examples and hadolint's write a `RUN … \ && …` chain. Every
;; instruction is a bracket that opens on its own line; a `RUN`'s command opens on the
;; same line and the two count once. A heredoc's lines are the script's, kept as written.
(def docker-indent-spec
  {:width 4
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets (conj docker-instructions :shell_command :json_string_array)
   :dedent #{}
   :opens #{"\\"}
   :verbatim #{:heredoc_block}})

(deflanguage docker
  {:lang :dockerfile
   :type :docker
   :pattern "(^|/)(Dockerfile|Containerfile)([.][^/]+)?$|[.]dockerfile$"
   :faces languages/docker-face-table
   :indent languages/docker-indent-spec
   ;; an instruction or a comment; a heredoc's lines may be anything
   :restart {:item-start "^([A-Za-z]|#)" :opaque "<<"}
   ;; C-M-a to the start of the instruction point is in — a long `RUN` chain's first line
   :definitions {:kinds languages/docker-instructions}
   ;; a `RUN` (or shell-form `CMD`) line is shell (brood ADR-489): its fragments, between
   ;; the `\` continuations, are ONE document, so a chain of `&&` lines parses as written.
   ;; A `RUN <<EOT` heredoc's body is shell too. Its marker sits inside the fragment and its
   ;; end inside the body, and both are left out of the shell (brood ADR-496 `:exclude`) to
   ;; stay Docker's strings: bash, handed a `<<EOT` with no body, parsed it as an error
   :injections [{:content {:kind :shell_fragment :parent :shell_command}
                 :exclude :heredoc_marker
                 :lang :bash}
                 {:content :heredoc_block :exclude :heredoc_end :lang :bash}]
   :grammar "https://github.com/camdencheek/tree-sitter-dockerfile"
   :fallback 'lex-dockerfile/dockerfile
   ;; no :format — Docker ships no formatter, and none is the community's standard
   :comment "# "})

;; ---- the web: CSS and HTML -------------------------------------------------------------
;; tree-sitter-css and tree-sitter-html, Emacs's css-ts-mode and html-ts-mode. An HTML page
;; carries the other two: a `<style>` element's text is CSS and a `<script>`'s is
;; JavaScript (brood ADR-489), each coloured with its own mode's table — and with HTML's
;; when that language's grammar is not installed.

;; A rule's selectors read as the heading of what follows, as a TOML table's header does,
;; so a tag, class, id, pseudo-class and the `&` / `*` selectors are functions; a property
;; is a keyword, as a JSON key is. An at-rule's `@media` is an ANONYMOUS token beginning
;; with `@`, which the keyword rule (a token beginning with a letter) cannot see — named
;; here, as C's `#include` is; an at-rule the grammar does not know is an `at_keyword`.
(def css-face-table
  (merge
    {:comment :syntax/comment
     :js_comment :syntax/comment
     :string_value :syntax/string
     :integer_value :syntax/number
     :float_value :syntax/number
     :color_value :syntax/number
     :important :syntax/special
     :property_name :syntax/keyword
     :feature_name :syntax/keyword
     :at_keyword :syntax/keyword
     :function_name :syntax/function
     :tag_name :syntax/function
     :nesting_selector :syntax/function
     :universal_selector :syntax/function
     ;; `.card` and `#main` whole — their `.` and `#` belong to the name
     :class_selector :syntax/function
     :id_selector :syntax/function
     :class_name :syntax/function
     :keyframes_name :syntax/function
     (keyword "::") :syntax/function
     {:parent :pseudo_class_selector :kind (keyword ":")} :syntax/function
     :attribute_name :syntax/special
     :from :syntax/special
     :to :syntax/special}
    (into {}
      (map ["@import"
             "@media" "@charset" "@namespace" "@keyframes" "@supports" "@scope" "@layer"
             "@container"]
        (fn (at-rule) [(keyword at-rule) :syntax/keyword])))))

;; Two columns, a level per `{ … }` and per bracketed argument list; a declaration continued
;; on the next line (a long `font-family:` list) indents its continuation, as prettier does.
(def css-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:block
               :declaration :keyframe_block_list :arguments :feature_query
               :parenthesized_query :selector_query}
   :dedent #{}
   ;; while a rule is being typed nothing parses, and a line ending in an opener is the one
   ;; hint left that the next line goes a level deeper
   :opens #{"{" "("}})

(deflanguage css
  {:lang :css
   :pattern "\\.css$"
   :faces languages/css-face-table
   :indent languages/css-indent-spec
   ;; a selector, an at-rule or a closing brace starts at column 0; a block comment above
   ;; may hold anything there
   :restart {:item-start "^([A-Za-z.#@:*&\\[]|\\})" :opaque "/\\*"}
   :format ["prettier" "--write" "--log-level" "warn"]
   ;; css-ts-mode's: C-M-a to the rule point is in
   :definitions {:kinds #{:rule_set :media_statement :keyframes_statement :supports_statement}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-css"
   ;; no line comment: M-; writes a prefix only, and a `/*` with no `*/` comments out the
   ;; rest of the file. `:comment-block` is the pair, for when M-; can wrap a line in one
   :comment-block ["/* " " */"]})

;; heex's table, which is HTML's with components: a tag name a keyword, an attribute's name
;; special, its value a string; the doctype and a character entity specials
(def html-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :doctype :syntax/special
   :tag_name :syntax/keyword
   :erroneous_end_tag_name :syntax/keyword
   :attribute_name :syntax/special
   :quoted_attribute_value :syntax/string
   :attribute_value :syntax/string
   :entity :syntax/special})

;; An element is a level and its closing tag backs out of it, as HEEx's and JSX's do; a tag
;; whose attributes run over several lines indents them. A `<style>` or `<script>` body is
;; another language's text, kept where its writer put it (`:verbatim`), as a heredoc is.
(def html-indent-spec
  {:width 2
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:element :style_element :script_element :start_tag :self_closing_tag}
   :dedent #{"</"}
   :opens #{}
   :verbatim #{:raw_text :comment}})

;; the text of a `<style>` is CSS and of a `<script>` JavaScript — the element's `raw_text`,
;; whatever its `type` (a `type="module"` script is JavaScript too)
(def html-injections
  [{:content {:kind :raw_text :parent :style_element} :lang :css}
    {:content {:kind :raw_text :parent :script_element} :lang :javascript}])

(deflanguage html
  {:lang :html
   :pattern "\\.(html|htm|xhtml)$"
   :faces languages/html-face-table
   :indent languages/html-indent-spec
   ;; no :restart — a column-0 `<` line may be inside a `<script>`, a `<pre>` or a comment,
   ;; and nothing on the line says which
   :injections languages/html-injections
   :format ["prettier" "--write" "--log-level" "warn"]
   ;; html-ts-mode's: C-M-a to the element point is in
   :definitions {:kinds #{:element :style_element :script_element}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-html"
   ;; a block comment only, as CSS's (`:comment-block`)
   :comment-block ["<!-- " " -->"]})

;; ---- C# (tree-sitter-c-sharp, Emacs's csharp-ts-mode) -----------------------------------
;; The grammar is named `c-sharp` — its library `libtree-sitter-c-sharp.so`, its symbol
;; `tree_sitter_c_sharp` — so `:c-sharp` is the language and `:csharp` the buffer type.
;;
;; A type's name, a predefined type, a generic's name, a base type and an attribute are
;; specials (Python's class name is one); a method, constructor or local function's name is a
;; function, and so is a called one — `Greet(…)`, and `WriteLine` in `Console.WriteLine(…)`,
;; told from a property read by its grandparent (brood ADR-487). An interpolated string
;; colours its text and quotes, not itself: what is inside `{…}` is code.
(def csharp-face-table
  {:comment :syntax/comment
   :string_literal :syntax/string
   :verbatim_string_literal :syntax/string
   :raw_string_literal :syntax/string
   :character_literal :syntax/string
   :string_content :syntax/string
   :interpolation_start :syntax/string
   {:parent :interpolated_string_expression :kind (keyword "\"")} :syntax/string
   :interpolation_brace :syntax/special
   :integer_literal :syntax/number
   :real_literal :syntax/number
   :boolean_literal :syntax/special
   :null_literal :syntax/special
   :predefined_type :syntax/special
   {:field :type :kind :identifier} :syntax/special
   {:parent :generic_name :kind :identifier} :syntax/special
   {:parent :base_list :kind :identifier} :syntax/special
   {:parent :class_declaration :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :interface_declaration :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :struct_declaration :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :enum_declaration :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :record_declaration :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :attribute :field :name} :syntax/special
   {:parent :method_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :constructor_declaration :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :local_function_statement :field :name} :syntax/function
   {:parent :invocation_expression :field :function :kind :identifier} :syntax/function
   {:kind :identifier
    :field :name
    :parent {:kind :member_access_expression :field :function}} :syntax/function})

;; Four columns, Allman braces: a `{` on its own line sits at its declaration's level (a
;; bracket counts only for the lines after the one it opens on) and its contents one deeper.
;; A `case` sits one deeper than the `switch`, its statements one deeper again — the body
;; is a bracket and so is each section. A raw or verbatim string's lines are its value.
(def csharp-indent-spec
  {:width 4
   :blocks #{}
   :brackets #{:declaration_list
               :block :switch_body :switch_section :switch_expression
               :accessor_list :enum_member_declaration_list :argument_list :parameter_list
               :initializer_expression :anonymous_object_creation_expression
               :parenthesized_expression :attribute_argument_list
               :bracketed_argument_list :type_parameter_constraints_clause}
   :dedent #{}
   :opens #{"{" "(" "["}
   :verbatim #{:verbatim_string_literal :raw_string_literal}})

;; a using, a namespace, a type or its attribute, a comment or a closing brace starts at
;; column 0; a block comment, a verbatim or a raw string above may hold anything there
(def csharp-restart-rule
  {:item-start "^((using|namespace|public|internal|private|protected|static|sealed|abstract|partial|class|interface|struct|record|enum|delegate|file|global)\\b|\\[|//|\\})"
   :opaque "/\\*|@\"|\"\"\""})

(deflanguage csharp
  {:lang :c-sharp
   :type :csharp
   :pattern "\\.(cs|csx)$"
   :faces languages/csharp-face-table
   :indent languages/csharp-indent-spec
   :restart languages/csharp-restart-rule
   ;; the .NET SDK's own: `dotnet format` works on a project, `csharpier` on a file
   :format [["csharpier" "format"] ["dotnet-csharpier"]]
   :definitions {:kinds #{:class_declaration
                          :interface_declaration :struct_declaration
                          :record_declaration :enum_declaration
                          :method_declaration :constructor_declaration
                          :property_declaration :namespace_declaration}}
   :grammar "https://github.com/tree-sitter/tree-sitter-c-sharp"
   :comment "// "})

;; ---- the configuration formats: tables over std's `editor/lexer` ---------------------
;; Fontify-only layers like env-mode; the lexing is `lex-configs`'. JSON, YAML and TOML are
;; parsed modes above, which keep these lexers as their `:fallback`s.
;; The `:file-pattern` matches the file's basename, so the extensionless names
;; (`Makefile`, `COMMIT_EDITMSG`, `.gitconfig`) trigger too. Line-oriented: each is a
;; `:line-lexer` (brood ADR-471) — stateless but for the commit message, which carries whether
;; its subject has been seen — so the pane keeps each line's spans and a keystroke lexes the
;; line it changed; the long-line slice path
;; restarts at the viewport's own line (`editor/highlight/line-restart`).

(deflexer makefile
  {:pattern "(^|/)(GNUmakefile|Makefile|makefile)$|\\.(mk|make)$"
   :lexer 'lex-configs/makefile
   :comment "# "})

(deflexer ini
  {:pattern "\\.(ini|cfg|conf|properties|gitconfig|gitmodules|editorconfig|npmrc|service|desktop)$|(^|/)\\.(gitconfig|gitmodules|editorconfig|npmrc|hgrc)$"
   :lexer 'lex-configs/ini
   :comment "# "})

(deflexer gitcommit
  {:pattern "(^|/)(COMMIT_EDITMSG|MERGE_MSG|TAG_EDITMSG|SQUASH_MSG|NOTES_EDITMSG)$"
   :lexer 'lex-configs/gitcommit})

;; git's pattern files, Emacs's git-modes (`lex-gitignore`): an ignore file — git's, and
;; the tools' that copy its syntax — and `.gitattributes`. `.gitconfig` and `.gitmodules`
;; are INI's, above.
(deflexer gitignore
  {:pattern "(^|/)\\.(gitignore|dockerignore|npmignore|prettierignore|eslintignore|stylelintignore|ignore|rgignore|fdignore|helmignore|gcloudignore|vscodeignore)$"
   :lexer 'lex-gitignore/gitignore
   :comment "# "})

(deflexer gitattributes
  {:pattern "(^|/)\\.gitattributes$"
   :lexer 'lex-gitignore/gitattributes
   :comment "# "})

;; ---- org-mode: outlines, TODOs and folding ---------------------------------------------
;; The text is `org`'s (src/org.blsp): the colouring and the outline facts (a heading's
;; level, where its subtree ends, the TODO cycle). Folding is the editor's
;; (src/folds.blsp) — this mode supplies what a fold covers (`:fold-extent`) and the keys.
;; Prose wraps, as in markdown: an Org paragraph is one long line.

(defn org-fold-extent (rope line)
  "Org's `:fold-extent`: the last line of the subtree under the heading at `line` of
`rope`, or nil when `line` is not a heading. Reads only the subtree's lines."
  (let (line-count (text/line-count rope)
        line-at (fn (index) (wrap/rope-line-text rope index)))
    (when (and (< line line-count) (org/heading-level (line-at line)))
      (org/subtree-end-by line-at line-count line))))

(defn- org-current-line (buf) (wrap/line-text buf (editor/buffer/buffer-current-line buf)))

(defn- org-replace-line (m f)
  ;; the current line replaced by `(f text)`, point keeping its distance from the line's end
  (let (buf (model/ed-current-buf m)
        line (editor/buffer/buffer-current-line buf)
        text (wrap/line-text buf line)
        start (text/line->char (editor/buffer/buffer-rope buf) line)
        from-end (- (string/length text) (editor/buffer/buffer-column buf))
        replaced (f text))
    (first (model/ed-try-edit m
             (fn (b)
               (editor/buffer/goto-char (editor/buffer/replace-region b
                                          start
                                          (+ start (string/length text))
                                          replaced)
                 (+ start (math/max 0 (- (string/length replaced) from-end)))))))))

(defcommand cmd-org-cycle
  (m key)
  "TAB in Org: on a heading, cycle its subtree FOLDED → CHILDREN → SUBTREE (Org's
`org-cycle`); anywhere else, indent or complete as TAB always does."
  (let (buf (model/ed-current-buf m)
        [cycled state] (folds/cycle-heading buf (editor/buffer/buffer-current-line buf)))
    (if (nil? state)
      (commands/cmd-indent-or-complete m key)
      (model/message (model/ed-set-buf m cycled)
        (case state :folded "FOLDED" :children "CHILDREN" "SUBTREE")))))

(defcommand cmd-org-cycle-buffer
  (m key)
  "S-TAB in Org: every heading folded (the overview), or everything shown."
  (let ([cycled state] (folds/cycle-buffer (model/ed-current-buf m)))
    (model/message (model/ed-set-buf m cycled) (if (= state :overview) "OVERVIEW" "SHOW ALL"))))

(defcommand cmd-org-todo
  (m key)
  "Step the heading's TODO keyword: none → TODO → DONE → none (C-c C-t, Org's `org-todo`)."
  (if (nil? (org/heading-level (org-current-line (model/ed-current-buf m))))
    (model/message m "not on a heading")
    (org-replace-line m org/cycle-todo)))

(defn- org-heading-from (rope line-count line step)
  ;; the nearest heading line from `line` in direction `step`, or nil — reading the lines it
  ;; passes, not the document
  (let (next (+ line step))
    (cond
      (or (< next 0) (>= next line-count)) nil
      (org/heading-level (wrap/rope-line-text rope next)) next
      else (org-heading-from rope line-count next step))))

(defn- org-heading-move (m step none)
  (let (buf (model/ed-current-buf m)
        rope (editor/buffer/buffer-rope buf)
        target (org-heading-from rope
                 (text/line-count rope)
                 (editor/buffer/buffer-current-line buf)
                 step))
    (if (nil? target)
      (model/message m none)
      (model/ed-set-buf m
        (editor/buffer/goto-char buf (text/line->char (editor/buffer/buffer-rope buf) target))))))

(defcommand cmd-org-next-heading
  (m key)
  "Move to the next heading, at any level (C-c C-n)."
  (org-heading-move m 1 "No next heading"))

(defcommand cmd-org-previous-heading
  (m key)
  "Move to the previous heading, at any level (C-c C-p)."
  (org-heading-move m -1 "No previous heading"))

(defn- org-change-level (m delta)
  (let (text (org-current-line (model/ed-current-buf m))
        level (org/heading-level text))
    (cond
      (nil? level) (model/message m "not on a heading")
      (and (< delta 0) (= level 1)) (model/message m "cannot promote a top-level heading")
      else
        (org-replace-line m
          (fn (line) (if (< delta 0) (string/substring line 1 (string/length line)) (str "*" line)))))))

(defcommand cmd-org-promote
  (m key)
  "One star fewer on this heading (M-<left>, Org's `org-do-promote`)."
  (org-change-level m -1))

(defcommand cmd-org-demote
  (m key)
  "One star more on this heading (M-<right>, Org's `org-do-demote`)."
  (org-change-level m 1))

(defcommand cmd-org-meta-return
  (m key)
  "A new heading at this one's level on the line below (M-RET); off a heading, a newline."
  (let (level (org/heading-level (org-current-line (model/ed-current-buf m))))
    (if (nil? level)
      (commands/cmd-newline m key)
      (let (at-end (model/ed-set-buf m (editor/buffer/end-of-line (model/ed-current-buf m))))
        (first (model/ed-try-edit at-end
                 (fn (b) (editor/buffer/insert b (str "\n" (string/repeat "*" level) " ")))))))))

(defn org-on-move (m)
  "org-mode's `:on-move`: point that lands inside a fold some way other than C-n / C-p
— a search, a jump — opens it, so the cursor is never in hidden text."
  (let (buf (model/ed-current-buf m)
        revealed (folds/reveal buf))
    (if (= revealed buf) m (model/ed-set-buf m revealed))))

(def org-keymap
  (-> {}
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:tab] 'languages/cmd-org-cycle)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:back-tab] 'languages/cmd-org-cycle-buffer)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:ctrl-c :ctrl-t] 'languages/cmd-org-todo)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:ctrl-c :ctrl-n] 'languages/cmd-org-next-heading)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:ctrl-c :ctrl-p] 'languages/cmd-org-previous-heading)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:alt-left] 'languages/cmd-org-promote)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:alt-right] 'languages/cmd-org-demote)
    (editor/keymap/keymap-bind [:alt-enter] 'languages/cmd-org-meta-return)))

;; the header line's crumbs for an Org buffer (`headerline/crumbs`): the headings point is
;; under. Memoised on the rope and the line, because the header asks every frame and the walk
;; up to a top-level heading is as long as the subtree.
(memo/defmemo org-crumbs-at
  4
  (rope line)
  (org/heading-path (fn (i) (wrap/rope-line-text rope i)) line))

(defn org-breadcrumbs (buf)
  "org-mode's `:breadcrumbs`: the titles of the headings point is under, outermost first."
  (org-crumbs-at (editor/buffer/buffer-rope buf) (editor/buffer/buffer-current-line buf)))

(def org-mode-layer
  {:name 'org
   :file-pattern "\\.org$"
   :fontify 'org/org-spans
   :line-lexer 'org/org-line-lexer
   ;; the band's first line, or the `#+BEGIN_` above it still open — not 200 lines of lead-in
   :fontify-restart 'org/org-fontify-restart
   :long-lines :wrap
   :fold-extent 'languages/org-fold-extent
   :breadcrumbs 'languages/org-breadcrumbs
   :on-move 'languages/org-on-move
   :keymap languages/org-keymap
   :commands (layer-commands languages/org-keymap)
   :comment-syntax "# "})

(register-language-mode 'languages/org-mode-layer :org)
